#!include:.gitignore

# The repository's own history is not build input. `.gitignore` does not list
# `.git` (nothing else needs it to), and `#!include:.gitignore` is the ONLY
# source of exclusions here, so without this line every deploy from a normal
# checkout uploads the whole object store to Cloud Build.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-15 in the shared checkout: 5,479 of 14,529 uploaded files —
# 38% — were `.git/` internals, ~172 MB. Found because a parallel session's
# deploy produced a 187.6 MB source zip and said so.
#
# Invisible from a git WORKTREE, which is the recommended way to deploy: there
# `.git` is a ~69-byte text pointer, so the upload looks clean and the problem
# only appears from the shared checkout. Two deploy paths, one of which cannot
# observe the defect.
#
# The Dockerfile never invokes git, and `scripts/write-build-commit.mjs` reads
# HEAD LOCALLY before the upload and ships the answer in `build-meta/`, so
# nothing downstream of this point needs the repository at all.
#
# VERIFIED both directions on a scratch tree with a real `.git/` directory:
#   without this line -> `.git/config`, `.git/refs/heads/main` in the upload set
#   with it           -> both gone, `src/` and the ignore files retained
.git

!backend/typescript/vendor/python-3.12.0.wasm
# Deploy provenance (ADR 0518 correction). `build-meta/commit.txt` is gitignored
# — deliberately, so `scripts/preflight-deploy.sh` (which refuses a dirty tree via
# `git status --porcelain`) does not trip on a file every deploy regenerates.
#
# But `#!include:.gitignore` above means gitignored ⇒ NOT UPLOADED, and this file
# has to reach Cloud Build or the Dockerfile's `COPY build-meta` lands an empty
# directory and every image reports `commit: unknown`. Caught pre-merge on
# 2026-08-10 by diffing `gcloud meta list-files-for-upload`: `.gitkeep` was in the
# upload set and `commit.txt` was not, while the file sat on disk.
#
# The failure would have been SILENT and test-invisible — the unit tests point at
# a temp dir via `OPENWOP_BUILD_META_DIR`, so they pass whether or not the real
# file ever ships. Same negation pattern as the python-wasm line above.
#
# THE NEGATION IS DIRECTORY-WIDE, and that is the correction (2026-08-15). It
# read `!build-meta/commit.txt` — one file — so when RFC 0146's
# `build-meta/corpus-suite.txt` was added it inherited the gitignore and was
# silently dropped from the upload. `contractProvenance` then deployed, again,
# ABSENT: the image had no stamp to read, the commit stamp matched HEAD, and
# `verify-deploy.sh` passed. The same defect, on the same day, caused by the
# same mechanism as the fix it was carrying.
#
# The per-file form encoded the INSTANCE. `build-meta/` is a purpose-built
# directory written only by `scripts/write-build-commit.mjs`, so every file in
# it is by definition a stamp that must ship — which makes the directory the
# correct unit and makes the next stamp file safe by construction rather than
# by remembering this line exists.
#
# MEASURED with `gcloud meta list-files-for-upload`, both directions, because
# the `**` form is not obviously valid under gitignore semantics (a negation
# cannot re-include inside an EXCLUDED directory — it works here only because
# `build-meta/` itself is never excluded, just files within it):
#   with    `!build-meta/**` → commit.txt, .gitkeep, corpus-suite.txt
#   without                  → commit.txt, .gitkeep        (corpus-suite drops)
!build-meta/**
